
#  Stack Notes

   push(x)  = Add element to top
   pop()    = Remove elements from top
   peek()   = Look at top element ( do not remove )

## Real life Uses of stack

   - Use Case	            How Stack Helps
   - Undo in editors     	Every action pushed; Ctrl+Z pops
   - Browser back button	Pages pushed; back = pop
   - Function calls	        Each function call is pushed on call stack
   - Bracket matching	    {[()]} validation uses stack

### Common Mistakes Make

    1. Popping from empty stack → always check is_empty() first

    2. Confusing index → top of stack is the last element ([-1]), not [0]

    3. Using pop from the wrong end → list.pop() removes from end ✅,
       list.pop(0) removes from front ❌ (that's a queue!)

#### Build a Stack from Scratch

    3 Ways to Use Stack in Java
    Option	                When to use

    Stack class (built-in)	Quick and easy
    Deque with ArrayDeque	Recommended in real projects
    Build your own	        Learning / interviews

   - Way 1 — Built-in Stack Class
     import java.util.Stack;

     public class Main {
         public static void main(String[] args) {

             Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

             // push
             stack.push(10);
             stack.push(20);
             stack.push(30);
             System.out.println(stack);        // [10, 20, 30]

             // peek — just look at top
             System.out.println(stack.peek()); // 30

             // pop — remove from top
             System.out.println(stack.pop());  // 30
             System.out.println(stack.pop());  // 20
             System.out.println(stack);        // [10]

             // check empty
             System.out.println(stack.isEmpty()); // false

             // size
             System.out.println(stack.size());    // 1
         }
     }

   - Way 2 — ArrayDeque (Recommended ✅)
     import java.util.ArrayDeque;
     import java.util.Deque;

     public class Main {
         public static void main(String[] args) {

             Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

             // push
             stack.push(10);
             stack.push(20);
             stack.push(30);

             // peek
             System.out.println(stack.peek()); // 30

             // pop
             System.out.println(stack.pop());  // 30
             System.out.println(stack.pop());  // 20

             // isEmpty
             System.out.println(stack.isEmpty()); // false

             // size
             System.out.println(stack.size());    // 1
         }
     }

  -  Way 3 — Build Your Own Stack

    public class MyStack {

        private int[] arr;
        private int top;
        private int capacity;

        // constructor
        public MyStack(int size) {
            arr = new int[size];
            capacity = size;
            top = -1;          // -1 means stack is empty
        }

        // push
        public void push(int item) {
            if (top == capacity - 1) {
                System.out.println("Stack Overflow! Stack is full.");
                return;
            }
            arr[++top] = item;
            System.out.println(item + " pushed");
        }

        // pop
        public int pop() {
            if (isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("Stack Underflow! Stack is empty.");
                return -1;
            }
            return arr[top--];
        }

        // peek
        public int peek() {
            if (isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
                return -1;
            }
            return arr[top];
        }

        // isEmpty
        public boolean isEmpty() {
            return top == -1;
        }

        // size
        public int size() {
            return top + 1;
        }

        // display
        public void display() {
            if (isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
                return;
            }
            System.out.print("Stack (bottom → top): ");
            for (int i = 0; i <= top; i++) {
                System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {

            MyStack s = new MyStack(5);  // stack of size 5

            s.push(10);      // 10 pushed
            s.push(20);      // 20 pushed
            s.push(30);      // 30 pushed
            s.display();     // Stack (bottom → top): 10 20 30

            System.out.println("Peek: " + s.peek());  // 30
            System.out.println("Pop: "  + s.pop());   // 30
            System.out.println("Pop: "  + s.pop());   // 20
            s.display();     // Stack (bottom → top): 10

            System.out.println("Size: " + s.size());  // 1
        }
    }

####   Two Key Errors to Always Handle
       Error	        When it happens	            What to do
       Stack Overflow	push() when stack is full	Check top == capacity - 1
       Stack Underflow	pop() when stack is empty	Check isEmpty()

#### Generic Stack (Handles Any Data Type)
     public class MyStack<T> {

         private Object[] arr;
         private int top;
         private int capacity;

         public MyStack(int size) {
             arr = new Object[size];
             capacity = size;
             top = -1;
         }

         public void push(T item) {
             if (top == capacity - 1) {
                 System.out.println("Stack is full!");
                 return;
             }
             arr[++top] = item;
         }

         @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
         public T pop() {
             if (isEmpty()) return null;
             return (T) arr[top--];
         }

         @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
         public T peek() {
             if (isEmpty()) return null;
             return (T) arr[top];
         }

         public boolean isEmpty() {
             return top == -1;
         }
     }

     MyStack<String> s1 = new MyStack<>(5);
     s1.push("hello");
     s1.push("world");
     System.out.println(s1.pop());   // world

     MyStack<Integer> s2 = new MyStack<>(5);
     s2.push(100);
     s2.push(200);
     System.out.println(s2.peek());  // 200

#### Reverse a String using Stack
     input:  "hello"
     output: "olleh"

     Why Stack fits here?
     Remember LIFO — last in first out.

     push h e l l o one by one
     pop gives → o l l e h

     That's your reversed string!

     Dry Run:-
     string = "hello"

     push all:
     h → stack: [ h ]
     e → stack: [ h, e ]
     l → stack: [ h, e, l ]
     l → stack: [ h, e, l, l ]
     o → stack: [ h, e, l, l, o ]

     pop all:
     pop → o
     pop → l
     pop → l
     pop → e
     pop → h

     result = "olleh" ✅

     Code:-
     public static String reverse(String s) {

         Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<>();

         // step 1 — push everything
         for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
             stack.push(s.charAt(i));
         }

         // step 2 — pop everything and build result
         StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
         while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
             result.append(stack.pop());
         }

         return result.toString();
     }

     public static void main(String[] args) {
         System.out.println(reverse("hello"));  // olleh
         System.out.println(reverse("abcde"));  // edcba
         System.out.println(reverse("java"));   // avaj
     }

